NNAAMMEE
       lvm - LVM2 tools

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       llvvmm [command | file]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       llvvmm  provides  the command‐line tools for LVM2.  A separate manual page
       describes each command in detail.

       If llvvmm is invoked with no arguments it presents a readline prompt  (as‐
       suming it was compiled with readline support).  LVM commands may be en‐
       tered  interactively  at this prompt with readline facilities including
       history and command name and option completion.  Refer  to  rreeaaddlliinnee(3)
       for details.

       If  llvvmm  is invoked with argv[0] set to the name of a specific LVM com‐
       mand (for example by using a hard or soft link) it acts  as  that  com‐
       mand.

       Where  commands take VG or LV names as arguments, the full path name is
       optional.  An LV called "lvol0" in a VG called "vg0" can  be  specified
       as  "vg0/lvol0".   Where a list of VGs is required but is left empty, a
       list of all VGs will be substituted.  Where a list of LVs  is  required
       but  a  VG  is  given, a list of all the LVs in that VG will be substi‐
       tuted.  So "lvdisplay vg0" will display all the LVs in "vg0".  Tags can
       also be used ‐ see aaddddttaagg below.

       One advantage of using the built‐in shell is that configuration  infor‐
       mation gets cached internally between commands.

       A file containing a simple script with one command per line can also be
       given on the command line.  The script can also be executed directly if
       the first line is #! followed by the absolute path of llvvmm.

BBUUIILLTT‐‐IINN CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
       The  following commands are built into lvm without links normally being
       created in the filesystem for them.

       dduummppccoonnffiigg — Display the configuration information after
              loading llvvmm..ccoonnff (5) and any other configuration files.

       ffoorrmmaattss — Display recognised metadata formats.

       hheellpp — Display the help text.

       ppvvddaattaa — Not implemented in LVM2.

       sseeggttyyppeess — Display recognised logical volume segment types.

       vveerrssiioonn — Display version information.

CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
       The following commands implement the core LVM functionality.

       ppvvcchhaannggee — Change attributes of a physical volume.

       ppvvcckk — Check physical volume metadata.

       ppvvccrreeaattee — Initialize a disk or partition for use by LVM.

       ppvvddiissppllaayy — Display attributes of a physical volume.

       ppvvmmoovvee — Move physical extents.

       ppvvrreemmoovvee — Remove a physical volume.

       ppvvrreessiizzee — Resize a disk or partition in use by LVM2.

       ppvvss — Report information about physical volumes.

       ppvvssccaann — Scan all disks for physical volumes.

       vvggccffggbbaacckkuupp — Backup volume group descriptor area.

       vvggccffggrreessttoorree — Restore volume group descriptor area.

       vvggcchhaannggee — Change attributes of a volume group.

       vvggcckk — Check volume group metadata.

       vvggccoonnvveerrtt — Convert volume group metadata format.

       vvggccrreeaattee — Create a volume group.

       vvggddiissppllaayy — Display attributes of volume groups.

       vvggeexxppoorrtt — Make volume groups unknown to the system.

       vvggeexxtteenndd — Add physical volumes to a volume group.

       vvggiimmppoorrtt — Make exported volume groups known to the system.

       vvggmmeerrggee — Merge two volume groups.

       vvggmmkknnooddeess — Recreate volume group directory and logical volume special
       files

       vvggrreedduuccee — Reduce a volume group by removing one or more physical vol‐
       umes.

       vvggrreemmoovvee — Remove a volume group.

       vvggrreennaammee — Rename a volume group.

       vvggss — Report information about volume groups.

       vvggssccaann — Scan all disks for volume groups and rebuild caches.

       vvggsspplliitt — Split a volume group into two, moving any logical volumes
       from one volume group to another by moving entire physical volumes.

       llvvcchhaannggee — Change attributes of a logical volume.

       llvvccoonnvveerrtt — Convert a logical volume from linear to mirror or snapshot.

       llvvccrreeaattee — Create a logical volume in an existing volume group.

       llvvddiissppllaayy — Display attributes of a logical volume.

       llvveexxtteenndd — Extend the size of a logical volume.

       llvvmmcchhaannggee — Change attributes of the logical volume manager.

       llvvmmddiisskkssccaann — Scan for all devices visible to LVM2.

       llvvmmdduummpp — Create lvm2 information dumps for diagnostic purposes.

       llvvrreedduuccee — Reduce the size of a logical volume.

       llvvrreemmoovvee — Remove a logical volume.

       llvvrreennaammee — Rename a logical volume.

       llvvrreessiizzee — Resize a logical volume.

       llvvss — Report information about logical volumes.

       llvvssccaann — Scan (all disks) for logical volumes.

       The following commands are not implemented in LVM2 but might be in the
       future: lvmsadc, lvmsar, pvdata.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       The following options are available for many of the commands.  They are
       implemented generically and documented here rather than repeated on in‐
       dividual manual pages.

       ‐‐hh || ‐‐‐‐hheellpp — Display the help text.

       ‐‐‐‐vveerrssiioonn — Display version information.

       ‐‐vv || ‐‐‐‐vveerrbboossee — Set verbose level.
              Repeat from 1 to 3 times to increase the detail of messages sent
              to stdout and stderr.  Overrides config file setting.

       ‐‐dd || ‐‐‐‐ddeebbuugg — Set debug level.
              Repeat from 1 to 6 times to increase the detail of messages sent
              to the log file and/or syslog (if configured).  Overrides config
              file setting.

       ‐‐‐‐qquuiieett — Suppress output and log messages.
              Overrides ‐d and ‐v.

       ‐‐tt || ‐‐‐‐tteesstt — Run in test mode.
              Commands will not update metadata.  This is implemented by  dis‐
              abling  all  metadata writing but nevertheless returning success
              to the calling function.  This may lead to  unusual  error  mes‐
              sages in multi‐stage operations if a tool relies on reading back
              metadata it believes has changed but hasn’t.

       ‐‐‐‐ddrriivveerrllooaaddeedd { yy | nn }
              Whether  or  not  the device‐mapper kernel driver is loaded.  If
              you set this to nn, no attempt will be made to contact  the  dri‐
              ver.

       ‐‐AA || ‐‐‐‐aauuttoobbaacckkuupp { yy | nn }
              Whether or not to metadata should be backed up automatically af‐
              ter  a  change.   You  are strongly advised not to disable this!
              See vvggccffggbbaacckkuupp ((88))..

       ‐‐PP || ‐‐‐‐ppaarrttiiaall
              When set, the tools will do their best to provide access to vol‐
              ume groups that are only partially available.  Where part  of  a
              logical volume is missing, //ddeevv//iiooeerrrroorr will be substituted, and
              you  could  use  ddmmsseettuupp ((88)) to set this up to return I/O errors
              when accessed, or create it as a large block  device  of  nulls.
              Metadata  may  not  be changed with this option. To insert a re‐
              placement physical volume of the same or large size use ppvvccrreeaattee
              ‐‐uu to set the uuid to match the original  followed  by  vvggccffggrree‐‐
              ssttoorree ((88)).

       ‐‐MM || ‐‐‐‐mmeettaaddaattaattyyppee ttyyppee
              Specifies which type of on‐disk metadata to use, such as llvvmm11 or
              llvvmm22,  which can be abbreviated to 11 or 22 respectively.  The de‐
              fault (lvm2) can be changed by setting ffoorrmmaatt in the gglloobbaall sec‐
              tion of the config file.

       ‐‐‐‐iiggnnoorreelloocckkiinnggffaaiilluurree
              This lets you proceed with read‐only metadata operations such as
              llvvcchhaannggee ‐‐aayy and vvggcchhaannggee ‐‐aayy even if the locking module  fails.
              One  use  for this is in a system init script if the lock direc‐
              tory is mounted read‐only when the script runs.

       ‐‐‐‐aaddddttaagg ttaagg
              Add the tag ttaagg to a PV, VG or LV.  A tag is a word that can  be
              used  to group LVM2 objects of the same type together.  Tags can
              be given on the command line in place of PV, VG or LV arguments.
              Tags should be prefixed with @ to avoid ambiguity.  Each tag  is
              expanded  by  replacing  it with all objects possessing that tag
              which are of the type expected by its position  on  the  command
              line.  PVs can only possess tags while they are part of a Volume
              Group:  PV  tags are discarded if the PV is removed from the VG.
              As an example, you could tag some LVs as ddaattaabbaassee and others  as
              uusseerrddaattaa  and  then activate the database ones with llvvcchhaannggee ‐‐aayy
              @@ddaattaabbaassee.  Objects can possess  multiple  tags  simultaneously.
              Only  the new LVM2 metadata format supports tagging: objects us‐
              ing the LVM1 metadata format cannot be tagged  because  the  on‐
              disk  format  does  not support it.  Snapshots cannot be tagged.
              Characters allowed in tags are: A‐Z a‐z 0‐9 _ + . ‐

       ‐‐‐‐ddeellttaagg ttaagg
              Delete the tag ttaagg from a PV, VG or LV, if it’s present.

       ‐‐‐‐aalllloocc AAllllooccaattiioonnPPoolliiccyy
              The allocation policy to use: ccoonnttiigguuoouuss,  cclliinngg,  nnoorrmmaall,  aannyy‐‐
              wwhheerree or iinnhheerriitt.  When a command needs to allocate physical ex‐
              tents  from the volume group, the allocation policy controls how
              they are chosen.  Each volume group and logical  volume  has  an
              allocation  policy.   The  default  for a volume group is nnoorrmmaall
              which applies common‐sense rules such as  not  placing  parallel
              stripes  on the same physical volume.  The default for a logical
              volume is iinnhheerriitt which applies the same policy as for the  vol‐
              ume group.  These policies can be changed using llvvcchhaannggee (8) and
              vvggcchhaannggee  (8)  or over‐ridden on the command line of any command
              that performs allocation.  The ccoonnttiigguuoouuss policy  requires  that
              new  extents  be placed adjacent to existing extents.  The cclliinngg
              policy places new extents on the same physical volume as  exist‐
              ing  extents in the same stripe of the Logical Volume.  If there
              are sufficient free extents to satisfy an allocation request but
              nnoorrmmaall doesn’t use them, aannyywwhheerree will ‐ even  if  that  reduces
              performance by placing two stripes on the same physical volume.

              N.B. The policies described above are not implemented fully yet.
              In particular, contiguous free space cannot be broken up to sat‐
              isfy allocation attempts.

